1. Title
Subject-Disjoint Evaluation and Modern Deep Learning Benchmarks for the Bangladesh
Common Skin Disease Image Dataset.
2. Introduction
Applications
Clinical triage and primary care a validated, per-class screening classifier can assist
primary care physicians and community health workers in Bangladesh, where specialist
dermatologists are scarce, by flagging images that require referral rather than treating
every case as urgent.
Tele-dermatology and mHealth smartphone-captured clinical images are the natural input
for remote consultation; a classifier evaluated clinical (non-dermoscopic) photographs
directly support such services.
Public health surveillance automated, consistent annotation of common conditions
(contact dermatitis, eczema, scabies, tinea ringworm, vitiligo) can support
epidemiological monitoring of skin disease burden.
Methodological audit and reproducibility of the subject-disjoint evaluation protocol
proposed here generalizes other augmented medical-image datasets, giving the
community a template for leakage safe benchmarking.
Challenges
Very small raw samples roughly 197-250 unique patient photographs across five classes;
all reported results rest on augmented derivatives of these originals.
Leakage-prone augmentation in the released set contains 1,612 images, so a naive
random split mixes augmented copies of the same photograph into both training and test
partitions, inflating accuracy.
Class imbalance per-class image counts range from 302 to 381, favoring the majority of
classes under overall accuracy reporting.
Clinical imaging conditions variable smartphone cameras, lighting, background clutter,
and skin tones; images are clinical photographs, not standardized dermoscopy.
Absence of metadata with no patient-level attributes are provided, which precludes
subgroup and fairness analyses within this study.
Motivation
The dataset was released with a benchmarking study that reports very high classification
accuracy on the augmented image set [1]. Because thousands of images are derived from
only a few hundred originals, an image-level split almost certainly admits subject-level
leakage: augmented derivatives of the same raw photograph appear in both training and
test partitions, hiding per-class failures and overstating performance. This flaw is
common in augmented medical-image dataset papers and undermines clinical credibility.
At the same time, no modern deep-learning baselines pretrained transfer learning,
EfficientNet-family CNNs, or Vision Transformers have been evaluated on this dataset,
and no per-class triage statistics have been reported. These three gaps (leakage, missing
baselines, missing per-class reporting) define the scope of this proposal.
Problem Statement
This research addresses the following problem: construct a subject-disjoint (raw-image-
level) evaluation protocol for the Bangladesh Common Skin Disease Image Dataset;
establish the first modern deep-learning benchmark suite for it, covering convolutional
(ResNet-50, DenseNet-121, EfficientNet-B0/B3, MobileNetV3) and transformer (ViT-
B/16, Swin-T) architectures under transfer learning; and report clinically meaningful per-
class triage metrics sensitivity, specificity, confusion patterns together with a training-
trick ablation (MixUp/CutMix, focal loss, class-balanced sampling, label smoothing, test-
time augmentation) that addresses class imbalance.
3. Literature Review
