Since the release of HAM10000 [2], the field of automated skin-disease diagnosis has
converged on standardized dermoscopic benchmarks: large, curated collections of
dermoscopic images used in the ISIC challenges, where winning entries combine strong
pretrained convolutional architectures, multi-scale ensembling, and aggressive
augmentation [3]. These advances, however, are almost exclusively validated on light-
skinned, dermoscopic imagery and presume dataset sizes far beyond what Bangladeshi
clinical repositories offer.
Transformer-based models have matched or surpassed convolutional baselines on these
benchmarks. A Swin Transformer reached 97.2% accuracy and 82.3% balanced accuracy
on ISIC 2019 [4] evidence that attention-based models are viable for skin lesion
classification, but leaving open whether they generalize to small, imbalanced, clinical-
photo datasets such as ours (note that the 97.2% headline accuracy collapses to 82.3%
balanced accuracy once class imbalance is accounted for).
For imbalanced and high-variance skin-lesion data, loss function engineering is the
established remedy. The SCTFD framework [5] combines synthetic minority
oversampling (CN-SMOTE), a multi-scale attention network (MARD-Net), and a
Focal+Dice loss with intra-class variance regularization, achieving state-of-the-art results
on ISIC 2018/2019 with recall gains of up to 6.1% over the second-best method.
At the regional level, two Bangladeshi clinical datasets now exist. The target dataset of
this proposal [1] contains 1,612 images (about 197-250 raw) of five common diseases
collected at Faridpur Medical College. SkinDisNet [6] provides 1,710 patient annotated
images (416 patients, six classes) from two other Bangladeshi medical colleges,
overlapping four of our five classes (it has no vitiligo). Importantly, both releases
organize data into preprocessed/augmented folders, which invites image level split
leakage unless raw level grouping is enforced precisely the methodological gap this
proposal closes.
Research gap. In summary, the literature offers mature architectures [3][4], imbalance
remedies [5], and regional datasets [1][6], but no study combines subject-disjoint
evaluation, modern deep learning baselines, and per-class triage reporting on Bangladeshi
clinical skin images. This proposal occupies exactly that empty, clinically relevant niche.
4. Methodology
The proposed methodology follows a four-stage pipeline: (i) dataset acquisition and
leakage audit, (ii) subject-disjoint split and preprocessing, (iii) model development and
training, and (iv) evaluation, triage reporting, and explainability.
Dataset acquisition and leakage audit
The dataset (Mendeley v3, DOI 10.17632/9ggd3shdr7.3 [1]) is downloaded and audited.
The audit counts raw images (the repository lists 197; the paper abstract states 250; the
discrepancy must be resolved from the actual files), inspects folder and file-naming
conventions to determine whether each augmented image can be traced to its raw parent,
and records the license terms before any redistribution. All images are already
anonymized by the dataset authors; this study performs no new data collection.
Subject-disjoint split and preprocessing
If filenames encode the raw parent, a subject-disjoint stratified split (70/15/15) is
performed at the raw image level so that every augmented derivative of one raw
photograph stays in a single fold. If they do not, raw level groups are reconstructed by
clustering the 1,612 images with a pretrained embedding based near duplicate detector,
and the reconstruction fidelity is reported; as a fallback, the raw images themselves are
held out as a validation set. Images are resized to 224×224 (with a 512×512 variant to test
resolution sensitivity), normalized per model, and test-time augmentation is applied at
inference.
Model development and training
Eight pretrained models are fine tuned from ImageNet weights: ResNet-50, DenseNet-
121, EfficientNet-B0 and B3, MobileNetV3-Large, ViT-B/16, and Swin-T [3][4]. A
training-trick ablation runs from a baseline fine-tune through MixUp/CutMix, focal loss
(gamma = 2.0), class balanced sampling, label smoothing, and test time augmentation,
isolating each technique’s contribution. Every configuration is trained on three seeds and
reported as mean ± standard deviation, so gains are judged against variance, not cherry
picked runs.
Evaluation, triage metrics, and explainability
Evaluation reports per-class sensitivity/specificity, precision/recall, macro and weighted
F1, and a confusion-matrix analysis identifying clinically critical confusions (for
example, eczema vs. dermatitis, and tinea vs. other scaly dermatoses), framed as triage
metrics for screening rather than a single overall accuracy number. The original
benchmark method [1] is reproduced under the subject disjoint protocol, and McNemar’s
test quantifies the accuracy of inflation attributable to leakage. Grad CAM heatmaps are
generated for correct and incorrect predictions of both CNN and transformer heads as
qualitative, discussion-level explainability; clinical validation of the saliency maps is
explicitly deferred to future work.
5. Conclusion
This proposal addresses the growing need for honest, reproducible, and clinically
meaningful automated diagnosis of common skin diseases in Bangladesh. It targets three
specific gaps in the released dataset and its benchmark [1]: subject level leakage that
inflates the published accuracy, the absence of any modern deep-learning baseline (CNN
or transformer) [3][4], and the lack of per-class triage statistics that hide which diseases
are confused in practice.
Expected outcomes include (i) corrected, leak free accuracy figures that robustly replace
the inflated published numbers; (ii) the first transfer learning and ViT/Swin benchmark
on the dataset; (iii) per-class sensitivity/specificity and confusion pattern analysis usable
in screening contexts; and (iv) a public reproducibility package of raw level fold
assignments, configurations, and code, making subject disjoint evaluation the community
standard for this dataset.
Limitations include the single center collection, the absence of metadata for subgroup and
fairness analysis, and the lack of clinician validation of saliency maps. Future work
extends the framework cross-dataset to SkinDisNet [6] for domain shift testing across
Bangladeshi clinics, explores privacy preserving synthetic augmentation with clinician
verification, and conducts a full explainability study.
